 ## Overview
-Half-Angle Slice Rendering is a technique for adding volumetric shadowing to particle systems by rendering particles as slices perpendicular to a half-angle vector (between view and light directions), allowing simultaneous accumulation of shadowing and blending using a single 2D shadow buffer [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently computing soft volumetric shadows for large numbers of particles without the memory overhead of 3D voxelization [S1, unofficial]. Unlike volume rendering approaches that require voxelization and 3D textures, this method uses only a single 2D shadow buffer and particle sorting [S1, unofficial].
+Half-Angle Slice Rendering is a technique for computing volumetric shadows in particle-based media by rendering the volume as a series of slices perpendicular to a "half-angle" vector between the view and light directions [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently accumulating shadowing information while blending the volume, allowing both operations to occur simultaneously using a single 2D shadow buffer [S1, unofficial]. Unlike voxel-based volume rendering methods that require 3D textures and complex ray marching, this method relies on sorting particles and 2D blending, making it suitable for real-time particle systems [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- Particle system data: positions $\mathbf{p}_i$, colors $\mathbf{c}_i$, alphas $\alpha_i$, and radii $r_i$ for $i=0 \dots N-1$.
-- View direction $\mathbf{v}$ (normalized vector from camera to scene, or camera basis vector).
-- Light direction $\mathbf{l}$ (normalized vector from particle to light source).
-- Number of slices $S$ (typical value: 32 to 128) [S1, unofficial].
-- Light buffer resolution $R_L \times R_L$ (typical value: 256x256 or less) [S1, unofficial].
-- Image buffer resolution $R_I \times R_I$ (can be downscaled, e.g., half or quarter resolution) [S1, unofficial].
+The method approximates the volume as a collection of point sprites (particles). The core innovation is aligning the slicing direction with the half-angle vector to ensure that the front-to-back order from the camera's perspective is roughly consistent with the front-to-back order from the light's perspective. This allows the accumulation of light transmittance (shadowing) to happen in the same passes as the color blending [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Outputs:**
-- Final image buffer with composited particles and volumetric shadows.
-- Light buffer (2D texture) storing accumulated transmittance/shadowing.
